/*****************************************************************************
    The floor: its rules, and the floor census, which is the drawing plan
*****************************************************************************/
The line between forms and drawing is the floor request: forms owns attributes, layout and the walk; drawing owns what happens under a floor method.
    C++ is only the short drawing floor. Each floor method takes ONE argument, a request group built fresh per call (textReq, imageReq); results are written into the request; a failure refuses by name and draws nothing.
    The yardstick: each step adds at most one floor method, and prefers retiring to adding.
    Core Text measures and Core Graphics draws, to the screen and to PDF. No libharu.
    Foreign calls, shape (C) (Tony, 2026-11-07): groups.ext records each floor method's typed parameters so the jitter can later emit a direct typed call. No multi-argument call syntax.
    The drawing language moves into kant when the jitter allows (A5). A small drawing language is a parked design question; it may not be needed if colour and font stay with styles (SEQ 582 R6).
    Evidence: the drawing fields at the top of Topics/forms (WorkingOn/drawing retired 2026-11-12, git ede70292); docs/bindingmethods.md.

